avapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Audit complete. The corrected guidance matches rebar's documentation policy only when concision is judged against the artifact's reader, purpose, and lifecycle; it is not a universal ticket-length rule.\n\nProject mapping:\n- Current-state internal docs, the docs index, shipped help, templates, and code comments normally follow it: correct in place, lead with the usable contract, cite rather than copy history, and route detail progressively (docs/documentation-policy.md:13-16,22,30; docs/README.md:60-141).\n- AGENTS.md partially follows. Its declared design is lean launch context plus one-line pointers (lines 10-15,181-250), but the current 509-line file also embeds dated incident narrative and operator-recovery detail (252-330). Exact high-risk commands and repeated point-of-action safety constraints are justified; bulky history is a future move/link candidate.\n- Executable ticket descriptions/plans normally follow directly: short problem, one chosen path, grounded scope, measurable criteria, and verification (src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md:75-90,118-138). The line-241 “weigh alternatives” coaching move is temporary planner/reviewer reasoning: the G6 rubric explicitly says generate and DISCARD alternatives and retain one implementation path (src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_G6.md:10). Hard-to-reverse rationale belongs in an ADR.\n- Ticket comments/handoffs follow when they report only a delta, proof, blocker, decision, and exact next action. The compiled ticket intentionally retains corrections because events are append-only; retained history is not bloat, repeated recap is.\n- ADRs intentionally diverge: they retain context, serious alternatives, rejection reasons, consequences, reversibility, and supersession. docs/adr/0018-plan-review-trust-boundary-scope-gate.md:31-92 is representative.\n- User/operator docs follow the general minimum-sufficient contract but not ticket terseness. They need prerequisites, ordered steps, examples, expected results, environment alternatives, warnings at the point of risk, and likely-failure troubleshooting. docs/user-guide.md:11-75 demonstrates the primary path; docs/dependency-advisory-runbook.md:47-105 deliberately preserves failed remedies to prevent unsafe/costly rediscovery.\n- Reference/API docs intentionally diverge from ticket density: arbitrary-entry readers need complete parameters, defaults, errors, compatibility, edge cases, and some local repetition. Many are generated from canonical registries and must be corrected through their generators, not hand-compressed (docs/generated-artifacts.md:1-31; docs/mcp-reference.md:1-35).\n- Security material splits by role. docs/security.md is generated current reference; the dependency-advisory page is a living runbook. Actual incident/security evidence should retain chronology, exact safe errors, hypotheses, negative results, mitigations, hashes, and residual risk.\n- Historical validation/protected evidence intentionally diverges. docs/88ab-feature-branch-evidence.md:1-80 repeats change IDs, run IDs, pre/post state, and criterion-level proof because reproducibility is the purpose. Corrections are adjacent, not destructive.\n- session_log and research notes intentionally diverge. session_log is defined as “verbose, durable, agent-facing,” lifecycle/gate exempt, hidden from normal work queues, and never Jira-synced (docs/event-schema.md:146-177). It should have a concise summary/index, but may retain chronology, hypotheses, and raw evidence.\n- Licenses, quotations, captures, frozen output, serialized fixtures, generated outputs, historical ticket events, ADR substance, and protected evidence are excluded from ordinary prose rewriting (docs/documentation-policy.md:69-93).\n\nObserved ticket practice is mixed but supports this classification. In the deliberately long 20-ticket sample, 14 were appropriate and 6 bloated. The six defects were current-layer defects (revision residue, cross-section echo, or truncated acceptance residue), not mere length. S1-S3 perfectly fit that discovery sample but remain retrospective, not production-approved.\n\nDefect boundary: detail is bloat when it serves the wrong lifecycle or repeats retrievable material; it is necessary when it enables execution, decision revisit, user success, arbitrary-entry lookup, safety, audit, or reproduction.\n\nArtifacts:\n- session_log 59a7-cb88-f2f9-4148 (this audit appended)\n- this ticket comment\n- final user answer\n- no repository files","timestamp":1788499590905461001}],"completion_expectation":"required","created_at":1788498575274680458,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"bf818157-6237-4539-b345-185393d10ee5","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"59a7-cb88-f2f9-4148"}],"description":"## Context\nAudit how the artifact-specific concision guidance recorded by inanimate-unrideable-wasp maps to documentation and durable records in the current rebar project.\n\n## Approach\n- Read the canonical documentation policy and indexes, then inspect representative examples of agent guidance, executable-plan guidance, ADRs, user-facing guides, generated reference/API documentation, incident/security material, templates/prompts, ticket comments, and session logs.\n- For each artifact class, compare its stated purpose and observed structure with the proposed contract: minimum sufficient content, progressive disclosure, protected facts, current-versus-historical state, alternatives, evidence, and resumption information.\n- Classify each class as typically following, partially following, or intentionally not following the guidance. Describe concrete deviations and whether they are defects or purpose-driven exceptions.\n- Cite exact repository files and use the existing historical-ticket calibration for tracker artifacts.\n\n## Artifacts\n- A concise answer with repository citations.\n- A ticket comment recording the audit result.\n- No repository files.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The answer covers canonical agent guidance, executable tickets/plans, comments/handoffs, ADRs, user-facing documentation, reference/API documentation, incident/security records, and session logs/research notes.\n- [x] Each artifact class has a follows/partially follows/does-not-follow classification.\n- [x] Each non-following or partial class names the specific ways it diverges.\n- [x] Purpose-driven exceptions are distinguished from bloat defects.\n- [x] Claims about repository documentation cite current files.\n- [x] No repository files are modified.\n\n## Verification\nCross-check classifications against docs/documentation-policy.md, docs/README.md, representative files from each artifact class, and the completed 20-ticket calibration.","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"none","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","managed_refs":[["relates_to","59a7-cb88-f2f9-4148"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"Read-only audit of repository documentation and existing ticket/session-log artifacts; results are recorded in rebar and the response.","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"b6c97038-6bd2-472f-9e50-d477417c5bdf","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":["research","documentation","writing-quality"],"ticket_id":"3960-1016-ad4b-4c90","ticket_type":"task","title":"Audit concision guidance against current project artifacts","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["085de735-2af0-47ec-8b7c-c6c4a8837034","358380e3-f465-4256-9311-d3ad3eeba056","5be07a06-0394-43c3-98ec-168c256eccb8","1c871d6e-2efe-46e1-952a-c70a369537cf","6eb62e2a-3568-4c13-a76b-b936e9b6d725","bf818157-6237-4539-b345-185393d10ee5","3497cffe-3ac5-4712-944a-9ce5495b27bc","3c52ddaa-3e73-438b-bb92-c1a82a8b5cd3","839b74ec-1dd4-4fb7-8afc-0896dc9f0ad9","b6c97038-6bd2-472f-9e50-d477417c5bdf","d00b3338-f25e-4406-8c71-ada